DOCKER

Docker - Containerization tool that became open source in 2013
Combination of Application and Configuration
Docker Containers:
Isolation: Containers isolate the application and its dependencies from the underlying system. This allows you to run applications with consistent behavior, regardless of the environment they are deployed in (e.g., development, testing, or production).
Lightweight: Unlike virtual machines (VMs), containers share the host operating system's kernel, making them more efficient and faster to start up than VMs.
Portability: You can create a container image (a snapshot of the application and its environment) that can be run on any machine with Docker installed, regardless of the underlying OS or hardware.
Dockerfile: A text file that contains instructions to build a Docker image. It specifies what software and dependencies the container needs and how to set up the environment.
Image vs. Container:
Docker Image: A read-only template that includes the operating system, software, and application configurations.
Docker Container: A running instance of a Docker image. Containers can be started, stopped, moved, and deleted.
Virtualization allows you to create virtual versions of physical resources like servers, storage, or operating systems. It enables multiple virtual machines (VMs) to run on a single physical server, each with its own OS and applications.
Key Concepts:
Hypervisor: Software that manages VMs. It sits between the physical hardware and the virtual machines. There are two types:
Type 1 (Bare-metal): Runs directly on physical hardware (e.g., VMware ESXi).
Type 2 (Hosted): Runs on top of an existing operating system (e.g., VirtualBox).
Virtual Machine (VM): A fully isolated environment that acts like a real computer. Each VM runs its own OS and software but shares physical resources like CPU, memory, and storage.

Installing Docker

Windows 10/11 64-bit at least 4GB of RAM
Updated version of Windows Subsystem for Linux 2, Hypervisor/Hyper-V enabled
Enable it via "Turn Windows features on or off", restart your PC, and use Hyper-V Manager to create, manage, and run virtual OS instances (e.g., Windows/Linux) by assigning virtual resources like RAM, disk, and network
Install Docker from any browser desktop for windows-x86-AMD

Docker Client - VS Code - allows you to perform various operations on the command line
Docker Daemon - Docker Desktop - Docker’s Engine - locally running engine
Docker Registry - Docker Hub - Public registry
Docker Image - application and docker file - Static, configurations of tools, information about the base image and language and current working directory, env variables, commands and libraries. All information about the application : how to run, what is there.
Building, tagging and checking it in docker desktop.
Collection of Running Images → Docker Container → Dynamic form of Image
Olumne is a storage space for your container, just like secondary storage
